{"author":{"id":"973fc03426eba9a243913e0f6e6823e102397744f60a9ab11c882af948c310c4"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1778869902,"nonce":"MtPujzovNm4Hquz9iVlx8PRMii0=","message":"The graph workspace should become Mandible's canonical surface for project structure and provenance, not just an activity visualization. It should let a user answer questions such as:\n\n- What work is active, blocked, merged, abandoned, or waiting for auth?\n- Which issues reference or motivate other issues across repos?\n- Which sessions worked on which issues, branches, commits, and provider specs?\n- Which artifacts came from which runs, analyses, reports, commits, and sessions?\n- Which provider can act on this spec/artifact/run/report?\n- Which subgraph should I inspect: issue dependencies, artifact lineage, provider runs, active work, or curated project semantics?\n\nExisting tabs should be treated as specialized projections. The graph workspace may eventually subsume parts of Issues, Work, Auth, Presets, and Sessions, but that should be a product decision made deliberately with MLL at implementation time. The spec should not require deleting useful dense list views.\n\n\nThe current GraphView is useful but narrow:\n\n- `src/types.ts` only allows `issue | session | auth_request` nodes.\n- `src/routes/graph.ts` assembles one fixed graph shape from IssueScanner, sessions, session_issues, and auth_requests.\n- Node and edge styling is hardcoded in `GraphCanvas.tsx`.\n- Repo filtering partly depends on path basenames rather than canonical repo IDs in all cases.\n- Node click navigates away; there is no persistent right detail pane.\n- Hover shows only a small tooltip; richer hover actions are tracked separately in `c1a886f`.\n- The API returns a capped mixed list after collection, which existing issue `78af187` notes can create type bias.\n- The graph has no first-class query model for subgraph type, expansion depth, pinned entities, semantic edge classes, clustering, provider capabilities, or artifact lineage.\n\nThese are normal prototype constraints. The right refactor is not to throw away the graph, but to separate graph data modeling/querying from the renderer and from one-off operational graph assembly.\n\n\nIntroduce a graph entity/edge model that can be backed by SQLite and rebuilt from source systems. A first durable form can be tables like:\n\n```sql\ngraph_entities (\n  id TEXT PRIMARY KEY,\n  entity_type TEXT NOT NULL,\n  repo_id TEXT,\n  provider_id TEXT,\n  external_id TEXT,\n  title TEXT NOT NULL,\n  status TEXT,\n  labels_json TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT '[]',\n  summary_json TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT '{}',\n  detail_ref_json TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT '{}',\n  action_context_json TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT '{}',\n  created_at INTEGER,\n  updated_at INTEGER,\n  observed_at INTEGER NOT NULL,\n  source TEXT NOT NULL\n);\n\ngraph_edges (\n  id TEXT PRIMARY KEY,\n  source_id TEXT NOT NULL,\n  target_id TEXT NOT NULL,\n  edge_type TEXT NOT NULL,\n  edge_class TEXT NOT NULL,\n  confidence TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT 'observed',\n  source TEXT NOT NULL,\n  metadata_json TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT '{}',\n  created_at INTEGER,\n  observed_at INTEGER NOT NULL\n);\n```\n\nThis table shape is illustrative, not mandatory. The required idea is that graph entities and edges become a shared indexing layer. Source systems remain canonical where they already are canonical: git-bug for issue bodies, sessions/conversations tables for sessions, auth_requests for auth, work_units for work lifecycle, provider manifests for provider outputs, and artifact records for artifacts.\n\nThe graph index should be rebuildable where possible. For derived edges from commit trailers, session_issues, auth request issue IDs, provider manifests, or artifact metadata, the source of truth is the source table/file. Curated semantic edges may be canonical in Mandible's graph tables.\n\n\nInitial first-class node types should include:\n\n- `repo`: registered project/repository from `work_repos`.\n- `issue`: git-bug issue, with repo identity and labels.\n- `work_unit`: Mandible work-unit lifecycle object.\n- `session`: Mandible agent session.\n- `conversation`: provider-native conversation or Mandible-ingested transcript.\n- `auth_request`: any reviewed action request, not only merges.\n- `branch`: branch/worktree identity when useful as a node, but not required in every graph mode.\n- `commit`: commit or merge commit, mainly for lineage and resolution edges.\n- `preset`: convention/preset node, per `6bbcf8a`.\n- `provider`: installed domain provider.\n- `provider_spec`: a planned or validated spec owned by a provider.\n- `provider_manifest`: emitted provider manifest.\n- `run`: provider run, training execution, evaluation, analysis, or report generation.\n- `artifact`: content-addressed artifact record.\n- `report`: human-facing or deterministic report product.\n- `curated_topic`: optional human/agent-curated project node that is not identical to an issue.\n\nThese should not all be required in MVP. The data model and renderer must, however, be extensible enough that adding them does not require rewriting the canvas.\n\n\nInitial edge classes should be separated into provenance, lifecycle, semantic, and provider-capability edges.\n\nProvenance/lifecycle examples:\n\n- `repo_contains_issue`\n- `issue_has_work_unit`\n- `work_unit_uses_branch`\n- `session_worked_on_issue`\n- `session_parent`\n- `auth_request_references_issue`\n- `auth_request_merges_branch`\n- `commit_resolves_issue`\n- `commit_produced_manifest`\n- `manifest_references_artifact`\n- `run_used_spec`\n- `analysis_used_run`\n- `report_used_analysis`\n- `provider_emitted_manifest`\n\nCurated semantic examples:\n\n- `blocks`\n- `depends_on`\n- `motivates`\n- `supports`\n- `contradicts`\n- `supersedes`\n- `duplicates`\n- `splits_into`\n- `merges_with`\n- `implements`\n- `tests`\n\nProvider/action examples:\n\n- `provider_can_open`\n- `provider_can_validate`\n- `provider_can_execute`\n- `provider_can_preview`\n- `provider_can_import`\n\nProvider/action compatibility may be computed dynamically rather than stored as durable edges. The graph API can include action descriptors on selected nodes without materializing every possible provider action edge.\n\n\nThe graph API should accept a mode and query object, not just `since/repo/branch/status`:\n\n```http\nGET /api/graph?mode=issue\u0026root=issue:mandible:0516fc0\u0026depth=2\u0026edge_class=all\nGET /api/graph?mode=provenance\u0026root=artifact:sha256:...\u0026direction=ancestors\nGET /api/graph?mode=provider-lineage\u0026provider=feedbax\u0026root=run:...\nGET /api/graph?mode=active-work\u0026repo=mandible\nGET /api/graph?mode=curated-project\u0026topic=...\n```\n\nRecommended modes:\n\n- `activity`: current behavior's successor; recent sessions/issues/auth requests.\n- `issue`: issue cross-references, umbrellas/children, blocking/supporting/superseding edges.\n- `work`: issues, work units, branches, sessions, auth requests, commits.\n- `repo`: repo-centered work state and recent changes.\n- `provenance`: specs, runs, commits, manifests, artifacts, reports.\n- `provider-lineage`: provider-specific specs/runs/artifacts with provider actions.\n- `curated-project`: semantic project map curated by humans/agents.\n- `preset`: presets, preset nodes, sessions, violations/diffs, and convention-setting links.\n\nModes should be declarative query profiles, not separate hardcoded routes. They can share the same entity/edge store and renderer while choosing different source adapters, default edge classes, layouts, and detail panels.\n\n\nA future graph query should support:\n\n- `mode`: named subgraph mode.\n- `roots`: one or more entity IDs.\n- `depth`: expansion depth.\n- `direction`: ancestors, descendants, both, or undirected.\n- `repo_id`: canonical repo filter.\n- `provider_id`: provider filter.\n- `entity_types`: include/exclude node types.\n- `edge_types` and `edge_classes`: include/exclude relationship classes.\n- `status`: status filter, with mode-specific interpretation.\n- `time`: relative or absolute range.\n- `limit`: node/edge limits with explicit truncation metadata.\n- `layout_hint`: force, layered DAG, radial, timeline, or provider-specified.\n- `include_actions`: whether to compute provider/action descriptors.\n- `include_previews`: whether to include hover-preview summaries.\n\nResponse shape should include more than nodes and edges:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"schema_version\": \"mandible.graph.v1\",\n  \"mode\": \"provenance\",\n  \"nodes\": [],\n  \"edges\": [],\n  \"clusters\": [],\n  \"actions\": [],\n  \"truncated\": false,\n  \"warnings\": []\n}\n```\n\n\nThe current Preact + D3 SVG implementation is acceptable for MVP and likely still best for the near-term graph workspace because it is already integrated, hackable, local-first, and sufficient for hundreds of nodes. Do not rewrite the entire Mandible UI stack just because the graph is getting richer.\n\nThe important refactor is to create a graph rendering boundary:\n\n- `GraphWorkspace`: owns query state, selected node, mode, filters, and detail pane.\n- `GraphCanvas`: renders nodes/edges/clusters and emits selection/hover/viewport events.\n- `GraphDetailPane`: renders selected entity details and actions.\n- `GraphPreview`: renders hover previews.\n- `GraphLegend`: data-driven node/edge visual language.\n- `graphTypes.ts`: shared frontend types matching backend graph payload schema.\n\nThe renderer should consume style/display hints from data where possible:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"display\": {\n    \"shape\": \"circle|diamond|square|hex|document|artifact|provider\",\n    \"color_token\": \"issue.feature\",\n    \"icon\": \"git-branch\",\n    \"size\": \"normal\",\n    \"cluster\": \"feedbax-runs\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\nHardcoded fallback styling is fine, but the system should not require editing `GraphCanvas.tsx` every time a provider adds a new node type.\n\nA WebGL renderer such as Sigma or Cytoscape should be considered only if real usage exceeds D3/SVG limits, especially if the graph needs thousands of nodes, smooth clustering, or heavy label management. If that happens, the data/query/detail/action contracts above should survive the renderer swap.\n\n\nThe graph workspace should support:\n\n- Hover preview: concise card with type, title, status, repo/provider, key metadata, and primary actions.\n- Click select: select node and open/update right detail pane, not necessarily navigate away.\n- Double click or explicit action: navigate to the specialized tab/detail view.\n- Right/detail pane: full issue/session/auth/artifact/provider-spec summary, relationships, timeline, and actions.\n- Expand/collapse neighborhood: add adjacent nodes by edge type/direction.\n- Pin/unpin nodes: keep important nodes visible across query updates.\n- Mode switching: preserve selected root where possible.\n- Search/add root: jump to issue ID, repo, artifact hash, run ID, session ID, provider spec, or provider name.\n- Lasso or multi-select eventually, useful for batch actions or curated edge creation.\n\nHover-action work in `c1a886f` remains useful, but for a generalized workspace hover actions should be generated from an action registry, not just hardcoded per issue/session/auth node.\n\n\nThe detail pane should reuse existing detail components where possible rather than duplicate them. For example:\n\n- Issue detail can reuse or factor out parts of IssuesView.\n- Auth detail can reuse AuthCard or a compact derivative.\n- Session/conversation detail can reuse Sessions/Conversation components.\n- Artifact/provider detail will need new components.\n\nThis is where product decisions matter. Some existing views may stay as list-first workflows; others may become alternate projections of the graph. When implementing this, ask MLL which views should remain separate, which should become graph modes, and which should be collapsed or de-emphasized.\n\n\nA practical first slice:\n\n1. Define `GraphEntity`, `GraphEdgeRecord`, and `GraphPayload v1` types with extensible metadata/display/actions.\n2. Replace hardcoded frontend graph node union with generic typed graph nodes.\n3. Add graph modes to `/api/graph`, initially `activity`, `issue`, and `work`.\n4. Move current graph assembly into an `activity` adapter.\n5. Add a right detail pane for selected issue/session/auth nodes.\n6. Use canonical repo IDs from `work_repos` consistently.\n7. Include truncation metadata and avoid post-collection type-biased limits.\n8. Add provider/artifact node placeholders only after provider/artifact records exist.\n\n---\nCo-Authored-By: Codex (GPT-5) \u003ccodex@openai.com\u003e","files":null}]}